---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import DocxToText from '@/components/tools/data/DocxToText.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="docx-to-text">
  <DocxToText client:load />

  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>How to extract plain text from a Word document</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>Drop one or more <code>.docx</code> files onto the page. Nothing is uploaded: the documents are unzipped and read in this tab.</li>
      <li>Choose the paragraph spacing. A blank line between paragraphs is easiest to read; one line per paragraph is easier to process with a script.</li>
      <li>Tidy the text: trim indentation, collapse runs of blank lines, and optionally straighten the curly quotes of Word.</li>
      <li>Copy or download. With several documents loaded, you can export all of them as one ZIP of <code>.txt</code> files.</li>
    </ol>

    <h2>What gets extracted</h2>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
      <table class="w-full min-w-max border-collapse text-sm">
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th class="border-b px-3 py-2 text-left font-semibold">Document part</th>
            <th class="border-b px-3 py-2 text-left font-semibold">Included</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td class="border-b px-3 py-2">Body paragraphs and headings</td><td class="border-b px-3 py-2">Yes, in reading order</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="border-b px-3 py-2">Bulleted and numbered list text</td><td class="border-b px-3 py-2">Yes: the text, not the bullet glyphs</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="border-b px-3 py-2">Table cell text</td><td class="border-b px-3 py-2">Yes, cell by cell</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="border-b px-3 py-2">Text boxes and shapes</td><td class="border-b px-3 py-2">Usually, when stored in the main document part</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="border-b px-3 py-2">Headers, footers, footnotes, endnotes</td><td class="border-b px-3 py-2">No: separate parts of the package</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="border-b px-3 py-2">Comments and tracked-change markup</td><td class="border-b px-3 py-2">No</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="border-b px-3 py-2">Images, charts, embedded objects</td><td class="border-b px-3 py-2">No: plain text cannot hold them</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h2>What a .docx actually is</h2>
    <p>
      A <code>.docx</code> looks like one file but is a ZIP archive. In it, <code>word/document.xml</code>
      holds the text as a tree of paragraph (<code>w:p</code>) and run (<code>w:r</code>) elements, with
      formatting stored separately. To extract the text, the tool unzips the package, walks that tree and
      writes one line break per paragraph. It uses the mammoth library, which is part of the page, so it
      works offline and needs no copy of your file.
    </p>

    <h2>Why the old .doc format is not supported</h2>
    <p>
      Word 97-2003 <code>.doc</code> files use a completely different container: a binary OLE compound
      document with the text spread across streams and piece tables. That needs a very different parser, so
      the tool detects the format and tells you, and does not give garbled output. Open the file in Word or
      LibreOffice, choose <strong>Save As → Word Document (.docx)</strong>, and convert that copy.
    </p>

    <h2>Straightening smart quotes</h2>
    <p>
      Word autocorrect replaces typed punctuation with typographic characters: <code>"</code> becomes
      <code>“ ”</code>, <code>'</code> becomes <code>‘ ’</code>, <code>--</code> becomes an em dash, and
      <code>...</code> becomes one ellipsis character. These look good in print but cause problems in a CSV,
      a JSON string or a code comment. The straightening option converts them back to ASCII and also
      changes non-breaking spaces to ordinary ones. A non-breaking space is invisible and can make a value
      fail to match in a lookup.
    </p>

    <h2>Common uses</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Moving a manuscript from Word into a plain-text editor or a static site.</li>
      <li>Preparing documents for search indexing, diffing or word-frequency analysis.</li>
      <li>Removing formatting before you paste into a CMS that would otherwise get the inline styles of Word.</li>
      <li>Extracting text from a folder of reports: drop them all at once and download the ZIP.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      To keep the structure, use <a href="/docx-to-markdown">DOCX to Markdown</a>, which keeps headings,
      lists, links and tables.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
